<h2>A dispute older than the law invoked against it</h2>
<p>Shivdei Automobiles became a tractor dealer for Sirsa and Fatehabad under a Dealership Agreement of 1 January 2002, preceded by a Letter of Intent of September 1998. The manufacturer claimed about Rs 1.29 crore in dues — including amounts from the gap period between the LoI's expiry and the 2002 agreement — and in June 2004 invoked Clause 25, appointing a retired Supreme Court judge as sole arbitrator, as the clause expressly permitted. The award of 9 January 2006 allowed the claim with 12 per cent interest. The Section 34 petition, heard two decades later, attacked the award on three fronts.</p>

<h2>The three objections and their fate</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Objection</th><th>Holding</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Unilateral appointment violates Section 12(5)</td><td>Rejected — Section 12(5) operates prospectively from 23 October 2015 and cannot reach a 2004 appointment made under an express contractual power; precedents invalidating unilateral appointments concern the post-amendment regime</td></tr>
  <tr><td>No notice under Section 21, so arbitration never validly commenced</td><td>Rejected — following the Bhagheeratha Engineering line, non-issuance is not fatal where no limitation consequence or prejudice is shown and the agreed appointment procedure was followed</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Gap-period dues fall outside the arbitration clause</td><td>Rejected — the arbitrator's finding that the LoI-based relationship continued until 2002, coupled with the clause preserving accrued financial liabilities, was a plausible view immune from Section 34 interference</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Why prospectivity matters</h2>
<p>The 2015 Amendment transformed Indian arbitration's approach to neutrality: categories of relationship listed in the Seventh Schedule now render an appointment void, and unilateral appointment clauses have been struck down in the modern case law. But the transformation has a commencement date. Awards rendered under the pre-2015 dispensation — often in long-running commercial relationships like dealerships, construction contracts and supply arrangements — are tested against the law applicable when the tribunal was constituted. A contrary rule would destabilise every concluded arbitration of that era and convert the amendment into a retrospective annulment machine, which neither its text nor the transitional provisions contemplate.</p>

<h2>Takeaways for commercial parties</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Date the objection:</strong> before attacking an appointment as unilateral, fix the date of appointment against 23 October 2015 — the entire argument turns on it.</li>
  <li><strong>Raise Section 21 points early:</strong> commencement objections carry weight when tied to limitation or demonstrated prejudice, and wither when raised for the first time at the setting-aside stage.</li>
  <li><strong>Scope objections need the contract:</strong> where successive instruments govern a continuing relationship, clauses preserving accrued liabilities will often carry pre-contract dues into arbitration.</li>
  <li><strong>Respect the plausibility line:</strong> Section 34 is not a first appeal; a reasoned construction of the contract, even if debatable, stands.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Parties still operating under old agreements with one-sided appointment clauses should not assume those clauses remain workable for new disputes — for arbitrations commenced today, neutrality requirements apply in full, and a fresh appointment mechanism by consent or under Section 11 is the safer course.</p>
</div>
